 "name": "Pollen, spores and seeds",
 "_note": "One generic biological airborne system, authored three ways, because pollen, spores and seeds differ in exactly three numbers and nothing else: how buoyant they are, how much of the wind they catch, and what shape they present to it. BUOYANCY is authored as a POSITIVE `gravity.y` against a high drag -- a grain lighter than the air it displaces rises, and the drag is what stops it rising forever, so the population finds a level and hangs there instead of either falling out or leaving. That is why none of these three has a fall speed: they do not fall, they drift, and the moment one falls it reads as snow. WIND is the coupling: `windInfluence` above 1 means the grain is essentially carried, which is what a spore is -- it has no momentum of its own worth the name -- while a seed at 0.8 keeps enough of its own motion to cross a gust rather than turn with it. SHAPE is ADR-370's leaf card: a seed is a card and tumbles (tumbleRate 1.6, aspect 0.30 -- long and narrow, a samara), a spore is a round dot, and pollen is the small round dot between them. sizeSkew 3.4 on the spores is what gives a fungal release its look: a great many almost invisible grains and a few that catch the light, rather than a uniform dusting. All three read the SAME wind field, so a gust crosses all three populations at the same moment and at the same place -- which is the thing that makes them read as one body of air carrying three kinds of cargo rather than three particle systems running side by side.",
